{"title": "Lab – Clinical activity score & Werner classification", "html": "<p class=\"muted small\">Source: CAS+ Werner.pdf – 1 pages</p><div class=\"slide\"><h3>Clinical activity score (CAS)- 1 point for each of the following items: <span class=\"sn\">#1</span></h3><ul class=\"sl\"><li>1. Spontaneous retrobulbar pain</li><li>2. Pain on attempted upward or downward gaze</li><li>3. Redness of eyelids</li><li>4. Swelling of eyelids</li><li>5. Redness of conjunctiva</li><li>6. Swelling of conjunctiva (chemosis)</li><li>7. Swelling of caruncle or plica</li><li>CAS &lt; 3- inactive GO</li><li>CAS ≥ 3- active GO</li><li>Werner classification (NOSPECS)</li><li>Class 0 (N) No signs or symptoms</li><li>Class 1 (O) Only signs, no symptoms (signs limited to upper lid retraction, stare, lid lag, and</li><li>globe lag)</li><li>Class 2 (S) Soft tissue involvement: periorbital oedema, irritation in the eyes, excessive</li><li>tearing, conjunctival congestion, or oedema (chemosis), enlarged lacrimal</li><li>glands; proptosis &lt;22 mm</li><li>Class 3 (P) Proptosis ≥22 mm+ soft tissue involvement</li><li>Class 4 (E) Extraocular muscle involvement—ophthalmoplegia; patients may complain of</li><li>diplopia due to ocular muscle dysfunction caused by either oedema during the</li><li>early active phase or fibrosis during the later phase (→and failure of muscle</li><li>relaxation, limiting the function of the antagonist muscle). Another common</li><li>finding is a convergence defect caused by infiltrative, fibrotic contraction of the</li><li>medial rectus.</li><li>Class 5 (C) Corneal involvement (keratitis) due to inability to close the eyes</li><li>completely=lagophthalmos (proptosis &gt;32 mm)</li><li>Class 6 (S) Sight loss (optic nerve involvement) ← likely due to ischemia of the nerve from</li><li>compression of the surrounding enlarged extraocular muscles</li></ul><figure><img data-src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"page 1\"></figure></div>"}